    <!-- Why rawhide is a problem -->
    <div class="prime-rap__section">
      <p class="prime-rap__section-label">The Problem</p>
      <h2>Why rawhide is not actually safe</h2>
      <p>Rawhide is the inner layer of animal hide — usually cow or horse. To make it shelf-stable, white, and odor-free, manufacturers treat it with sodium sulphide liming, bleach, and hydrogen peroxide. Some imported rawhide has tested positive for arsenic, lead, and formaldehyde residue.</p>
      <p>Beyond the chemical processing, rawhide has two mechanical risks: it softens as dogs chew it, and large chunks can break off and be swallowed whole. These chunks do not digest — they can cause intestinal blockages requiring emergency surgery.</p>

      <div class="prime-rap__warning">
        <p class="prime-rap__warning-label">&#9888;&#65039; FDA Warning</p>
        <p>The FDA has issued multiple warnings about Salmonella contamination in rawhide products. In 2017, a major recall affected over 30 brands. Rawhide is not a "natural" product — it is a chemically processed byproduct.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

