    <!-- Individual chew breakdowns -->
    <div class="prime-rap__section">
      <p class="prime-rap__section-label">The Breakdown</p>
      <h2>Each alternative, honestly assessed</h2>

      <div class="prime-rap__chew-grid">

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card prime-rap__chew-card--winner">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">&#127956;&#65039; Yak Chews</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge">Best Overall</span>
          </div>
          <p>Made from yak and cow milk, hardened through a traditional Himalayan process. Four ingredients. Fully digestible. The nub can be microwaved into a puffed treat.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Fully digestible, no splinter risk</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Single-origin, traceable ingredients</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Long-lasting for most dogs</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Not suitable for dairy-sensitive dogs</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Power-chewers may finish quickly</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">Bully Sticks</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge prime-rap__chew-card-badge--caution">Good, with caveats</span>
          </div>
          <p>Made from dried bull pizzle. Single ingredient, fully digestible, no splinter risk. High in protein. Main downsides: odor and calorie density.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Digestible, single ingredient</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Most dogs love them</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Strong odor</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; High calorie — watch intake</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Shorter duration than yak chews</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">Antlers</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge prime-rap__chew-card-badge--caution">Use with caution</span>
          </div>
          <p>Naturally shed deer or elk antlers. Very long-lasting. But antlers are hard enough to crack teeth — veterinary dentists frequently see slab fractures from antler chewing.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Extremely long-lasting</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; No odor</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; High tooth fracture risk</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Not digestible if swallowed</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">Nylon Chews</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge prime-rap__chew-card-badge--avoid">Avoid</span>
          </div>
          <p>Synthetic chews designed to last indefinitely. Dogs can shred off pieces which are not digestible and can cause blockages. Not a food product.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Very long-lasting</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Not digestible</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Shredded pieces cause blockages</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Synthetic — not a food</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">Rawhide</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge prime-rap__chew-card-badge--avoid">Avoid</span>
          </div>
          <p>Chemically processed hide. Bleached, treated with hydrogen peroxide, and sometimes contaminated with heavy metals. Chunks can cause intestinal blockages.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Cheap and widely available</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Chemical processing</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Blockage risk from chunks</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Salmonella contamination history</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prime-rap__chew-card">
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-header">
            <p class="prime-rap__chew-card-name">Rubber Toys</p>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-badge prime-rap__chew-card-badge--caution">Enrichment only</span>
          </div>
          <p>Durable rubber toys (Kong-style) are excellent enrichment tools, especially stuffed with food. They are not chews — they are toys. Use them alongside a real chew, not instead of one.</p>
          <div class="prime-rap__chew-card-pros-cons">
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Safe if intact</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-pro">&#10003; Great for stuffing</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Not a chew — no dental benefit</span>
            <span class="prime-rap__chew-card-con">&#10007; Replace when damaged</span>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <div class="prime-rap__faq">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="prime-rap__faq-item">
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-q">Are yak chews safe for puppies?</p>
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-a">Yes, with supervision. Puppies under 6 months should use the Small size regardless of weight, as their teeth are still developing. Always supervise and remove the chew when it gets small enough to swallow whole.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prime-rap__faq-item">
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-q">My dog has a dairy sensitivity — can they have yak chews?</p>
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-a">Yak chews are made from yak and cow milk, so they are not suitable for dogs with confirmed dairy allergies. If your dog has a mild sensitivity, the low lactose content (most is removed during processing) means many sensitive dogs tolerate them fine — but check with your vet first.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prime-rap__faq-item">
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-q">How do yak chews compare to bully sticks?</p>
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-a">Both are digestible single-ingredient chews. Yak chews last longer for most dogs and have no odor. Bully sticks are higher in protein but also higher in calories and have a strong smell. For most dog owners, yak chews are the better daily chew — bully sticks work well as an occasional high-value reward.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prime-rap__faq-item">
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-q">What is the puff trick?</p>
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-a">When your dog chews the yak chew down to a 1-inch nub, microwave it for 45&#8211;60 seconds. It puffs up into a crunchy, airy treat. This extends the value of every chew and gives your dog a satisfying finish to their session.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prime-rap__faq-item">
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-q">Are antlers really dangerous?</p>
        <p class="prime-rap__faq-a">Antlers are one of the most common causes of slab fractures (broken teeth) in dogs. Veterinary dentists consistently warn against them. The press test rule applies: if you press your thumbnail against a chew and it does not give at all, it is too hard for your dog&#39;s teeth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

